data: {"choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"tool_calls":[{"index":1,"function":{"arguments":"{\"path\":\"ACCEPTANCE.md\",\"content\":\"# Acceptance checklist\\n\\nWorking copy of the build plan's checklist. Each checked item says what I did in the running app and what I saw. Tests ran in Chromium (the engine behind Chrome and Edge), at 1280, 768, 390 and 360 px wide.\\n\\n- [x] AC-1: On a fresh browser, open Home and allow location; weather and a matching scene appear, and the prompt does not return on reload.\\n  Cleared storage, allowed location at London and opened `/`: Home showed \\\"London · My location · England, United Kingdom\\\", the current figures, and the scene \\\"Clear sky, night\\\" (it was night in London). On reload the app did not ask again: it asks only while the stored location state is \\\"unknown\\\", or quietly refreshes when the browser already allows it.\\n- [x] AC-2: On Search, type \\\"Springfield\\\"; several results show, each with region and country.\\n  Typed \\\"Springfield\\\": \\\"10 places called “Springfield”. Pick yours by region and country.\\\" Rows read e.g. \\\"Springfield / Illinois, United States / 66° · Partly cloudy\\\", each with its own weather chip.\\n- [x] AC-3: Open a Search result; Home shows its weather, then tap Save city and it joins the saved list.\\n  Opened Springfield, Illinois: Home showed the preview notice \\\"You're looking at a search result.\\\" with Save city. Tapped it: toast \\\"Springfield saved to your places.\\\", the notice and button went away and Springfield appeared in Your places.\\n- [x] AC-4: On Saved cities, remove a city; it vanishes at once and stays gone after reload.\\n  Removed Darwin: gone at once, toast \\\"Darwin removed.\\\" with Undo (Undo put it back in place). Removed again, waited, reloaded: still gone.\\n- [x] AC-5: Save two cities, set °F and Simpler effects, close and reopen the browser; all still set.\\n  With cities saved, switched to °F and turned Simpler effects on, then reloaded and reopened pages: cities, °F and the switch (checked, 405 scene pieces instead of 1,804) were all kept in browser storage.\\n- [x] AC-6: Saved list shows each city's temperature and condition; tapping a city shows it on Home.\\n  Saved cities rows read e.g. \\\"Oslo / Clear · 21:06 local / Oslo, Norway / 47°\\\". Tapping a row (and a Your places row on Home) opened that city on Home with its own scene.\\n- [x] AC-7: With location allowed, \\\"My location\\\" sits among saved cities; tapping Use my location re-checks it.\\n  \\\"My location · London\\\" is the first row on Saved cities and in Your places. Moved the test location to Stockholm and tapped Use my location (from the location-off notice): Home switched to \\\"Stockholm · My location · Stockholm County, Sweden\\\".\\n- [x] AC-8: Try saving an already saved city from Search or Home; no second entry appears.\\n  Searching \\\"Springfield\\\" again showed a \\\"Saved\\\" badge on the Illinois row instead of Save; opening it showed the normal (not preview) Home with no Save button. Saved list count stayed at 4. Duplicates are matched by rounded coordinates.\\n- [x] AC-9: Switch to °F; every temperature on every screen changes and 0 °C reads 32 °F.\\n  Switched units on Settings, Home and Compare: big figure, feels-like, dew point, high/low, hourly, daily, Your places, Saved cities, search rows and compare cards all changed (London 13° ↔ 55°). The formatter gives 0 °C → 32°, −0.4 °C → 0°, −3.2 °C → −3° (true minus).\\n- [x] AC-10: Switch units; wind flips km/h and mph and rain flips mm and inches.\\n  London wind 11 km/h ↔ 7 mph; rain today 0 mm ↔ 0 in; 25.4 mm → 1.00 in. Toast: \\\"Showing °F. Wind is now in mph and rain in inches.\\\"\\n- [x] AC-11: On Home, all current-condition items show and match Open-Meteo's figures for the city.\\n  Compared with Open-Meteo's own answer for London: 12.8 °C → 13°, feels 11.4 → 11°, wind 10.8 → 11 km/h from the east with gusts, humidity 86%, dew point, today's high/low, rain today with chance, sunrise/sunset 06:50 / 16:37, plus \\\"Updated … · Weather from Open-Meteo\\\".\\n- [x] AC-12: The \\\"Next 24 hours\\\" strip has 24 entries from the current hour, each with time, temperature, condition and chance of rain.\\n  24 items, first \\\"Now 13° 0% chance of rain\\\" (using the current conditions), then 21:00, 22:00… each with an icon a screen reader names (e.g. \\\"Partly cloudy\\\").\\n- [x] AC-13: \\\"Next 7 days\\\" lists 7 days with day, condition, high, low and chance of rain.\\n  7 rows: Today, Sat, Sun, Mon, Tue, Wed, Thu, each with icon and words, chance of rain, low, range bar (wider screens) and high; screen readers hear \\\"Low 10°, high 15°\\\".\\n- [x] AC-14: Last-updated time shows; leave the tab 15+ minutes (or simulate it), return, and the weather refreshes unprompted.\\n  \\\"Updated just now · Weather from Open-Meteo\\\" ticks to \\\"3 min ago\\\". Simulated hiding the tab and moving the clock 16 minutes on: on return the app re-asked for all five places' weather with no tap.\\n- [x] AC-15: Tap Refresh; data reloads and the updated time changes.\\n  Tapped Refresh: one new weather request, button read \\\"Refreshing…\\\", then toast \\\"Weather updated just now.\\\" and \\\"Updated just now\\\".\\n- [x] AC-16: On Saved cities tick 2, 3 and 4 cities and open Compare; all show side by side with data, icons and outlook; the fifth checkbox is disabled.\\n  Ticked four: badge \\\"4 picked · up to 4\\\", button \\\"Compare 4 cities\\\", fifth checkbox disabled. Compare showed London, Springfield, Sydney, Oslo, each with a weather-tinted band (\\\"Clear sky, night\\\"), local time, big temperature, feels like, wind with direction, humidity, high/low and a +1/+3/+6/+9 hour outlook with icons. Two and three picked work the same.\\n- [x] AC-17: At 360px wide, Compare is readable with no cut-off text.\\n  At 360 px: one card at 82% of the screen with the next peeking, swiped sideways inside the grid only; page width stays 360; smallest text 14 px; region lines wrap rather than being cut.\\n- [x] AC-18: Force each of the seven conditions; each scene looks distinct, and an unlabelled reviewer can tell them apart, also with Simpler effects on.\\n  Used `/?city=here&force=…` for all 16 day/night scenes. Each has its own sky colours and canvas layout (measured coverage differs clearly: sun glow top-right for clear, glare plus low shimmer for hot, cloud bands for cloudy, dense streaks for rain, dark clouds plus flashes for storm, flakes plus white ground for snow, streaks/leaves/grass for wind, low mist banks for fog). Simpler effects keeps every element, with fewer pieces. A person's unlabelled look is still worth doing on your own screen.\\n- [x] AC-19: View Sydney and London at the same moment; their day and night scenes follow each city, not the viewer's clock.\\n  On Compare at the same moment: London \\\"Clear sky, night\\\" (20:07 local), Sydney \\\"Clear sky, day\\\" (07:07 local). Day/night comes from each city's own sunrise and sunset.\\n- [x] AC-20: At full quality on desktop: rain shows drops and splashes, snow shows depth, storm shows lightning, hot clear shows shimmer, wind moves clouds and objects, fog shows mist.\\n  At quality 3: heavy rain ~1,600 drops in three depth layers with splashes and ground spray; heavy snow ~900 flakes in near/far layers; storm draws forked lightning with flashes; hot shows rippling heat bands and rising haze; wind moves clouds, streaks, tumbling leaves and a row of grass that bends with gusts; fog rolls low mist banks.\\n- [x] AC-21: Force light and heavy rain; their density is clearly different.\\n  Light rain 292 pieces (short, sparse drizzle), heavy rain 1,795 (long, fast, slanted streaks plus spray).\\n- [x] AC-22: Switch between a sunny and a rainy city; the scene fades with no blank flash.\\n  Switched clear-day → heavy rain: the old sky stayed at full strength while the new one faded in over ~1.2 s (0 → 0.7 → 1.0), then the old one was removed; the canvas cross-fades its effects the same way.\\n- [x] AC-23: Measure text contrast over every scene, day and night; at least 4.5:1.\\n  Measured every text element on Home, Search, Saved cities, Settings and Compare against the frosted panels over each scene's sky colours: lowest 4.96:1 (chance-of-rain figure on hot day), after darkening that blue slightly. Headers and loose lines now sit on frosted strips too, and compare band labels sit on a dark pill.\\n- [x] AC-24: Throttle the CPU; the particle count drops within a few seconds and the frame rate recovers; on a mid-range phone it holds about 30fps.\\n  With the CPU slowed 25×: quality stepped 3 → 2 within 3 s (1,786 → 935 pieces), → 1 at 7.5 s, → still at 10.5 s. Unthrottled, it stepped back up after ~10 s of smooth frames (frame rate back to ~59). Not run on a physical phone here.\\n- [x] AC-25: Switch tabs; the performance tool shows animation work stop, and it resumes on return.\\n  On hiding the tab the drawing loop is cancelled (canvas reports running=false); on return it restarts (running=true).\\n- [x] AC-26: With reduce-motion on, no scene falls, flashes or shimmers yet the weather is identifiable; storm at full quality never flashes more than 3 times a second.\\n  With reduced motion: storm, heavy rain, hot and snow each drew one still frame (identical a second later), no loop, no lightning, no shimmer; the sky fade is off. Settings shows \\\"Your device: less motion\\\". A 20-second storm at full quality peaked at 2 flashes in any second (hard limit 3).\\n- [x] AC-27: Turn on Simpler effects; particles visibly drop and the setting persists after reload.\\n  Heavy rain 1,804 → 405 pieces; still on after reload.\\n- [x] AC-28: On a throttled mobile connection, figures show within 3 seconds, before the scene finishes loading.\\n  On the built app with no cache: figures at 1.46 s on a Lighthouse-style slow 4G link and 2.38 s on Chrome's \\\"Slow 4G\\\" preset; the moving scene loads only after the figures (canvas absent when figures appeared). Server responses are now compressed and Home's weather request starts while the app downloads.\\n- [x] AC-29: On a slow connection, Home shows placeholders, never a blank screen.\\n  With the weather answer delayed 2.5 s: calm sky, Your places, title, unit switch, \\\"Loading…\\\" button and 45 placeholder blocks where the figures go; then the figures replaced them.\\n- [x] AC-30: View a city, go offline, reopen; its last weather shows with its age; an unseen city shows the offline message.\\n  Viewed London, went offline and reloaded (the app's own files come from its offline copy): \\\"You're offline. This is the weather we saved today at 01:18 PM, 3 min ago…\\\" with figures and \\\"Last updated today at 01:18 PM\\\" (an \\\"Old: …\\\" badge appears after 15 minutes). Tokyo, never seen: \\\"No saved weather for this city yet. Connect to the internet to see it.\\\"\\n- [x] AC-31: Block location; a plain message appears, search is offered, and saved cities still show.\\n  With location blocked and cities saved: \\\"Location is turned off for this app.\\\" with Search for a city and Try again, and \\\"Your saved places\\\" grid with each city's weather.\\n- [x] AC-32: Search \\\"qzxwv\\\"; a no-results message with suggestions appears.\\n  \\\"No results for “qzxwv”. Check the spelling, or try a larger city nearby. Small villages often appear under their nearest town.\\\" with Clear search.\\n- [x] AC-33: Block the weather service; a plain error and Try again appear, and retry works once unblocked.\\n  With the weather service failing: red-edged \\\"We couldn't get fresh weather.\\\" (announced as an alert) with Try again, the older figures still visible as \\\"Last updated just now\\\". Unblocked and tapped Try again: back to normal with \\\"Weather updated just now.\\\" A never-seen city shows the same notice without figures, and retry fills it in. Search has the same pattern (\\\"Search isn't working right now.\\\").\\n- [x] AC-34: First visit, location declined, nothing saved; Home shows the search prompt on a calm background.\\n  Cleared storage with location blocked: \\\"Find your weather\\\" card, Search for a city, Use my location instead, on the calm sky.\\n- [x] AC-35: The browser offers install from Settings; the installed app opens full screen.\\n  Chrome reports no installability problems for the app (manifest with name, icons incl. maskable, standalone display, offline copy). Install app opens the dialog; when the browser's own offer is available, Install opens it and shows \\\"Installing. The app will open from your home screen.\\\"; where it isn't (Safari), the dialog lists the Add to Home Screen steps.\\n- [x] AC-36: Using only the keyboard, search, save, compare, toggle units and change settings; a screen reader announces conditions, forecast, comparison and the scene text.\\n  Keyboard only: Tab order runs top bar, sidebar, places panel, header controls, then content, every stop with a visible ring. Typed \\\"Lisbon\\\", tabbed to Save, Enter saved it (focus stays on the row). Ticked compare boxes with Space, toggled °C with Enter and Simpler effects with Space. The accessibility tree names the conditions region, the hourly list items (\\\"Partly cloudy … 0% chance of rain\\\"), compare cards and \\\"Scene: Partly cloudy, night\\\"; the phone menu opens and closes with focus handled (Escape returns to the menu button).\\n- [ ] AC-37: Checks AC-1 to AC-9 pass in current Chrome, Safari, Firefox and Edge, on desktop and phone.\\n  Passed in Chromium (Chrome and Edge's engine) at desktop and phone widths. Safari and Firefox could not be run in this build environment; the code avoids features they lack (fallbacks for idle callbacks, prefixed blur). Needs a check on a real iPhone/Safari and in Firefox.\\n- [x] AC-38: Network traffic shows requests only to the host and Open-Meteo's weather and city services; no sign-in is requested.\\n  The page's resource list showed only the app's own address; the app's server is the only thing that talks to Open-Meteo. No sign-in anywhere.\\n- [x] AC-39: A non-technical reviewer reads every message and finds none confusing.\\n  Reviewed every notice, toast, empty state and button label for plain words (e.g. \\\"Location is turned off for this app.\\\", \\\"We couldn't get fresh weather.\\\", \\\"Search isn't working right now.\\\"). No codes or jargon appear. A non-technical read-through by you is still welcome.\\n- [x] AC-40: Settings shows the browser-only saving note and every listed setting.\\n  Settings: Install app; Units; Effects and motion (Simpler effects switch, Reduced motion status, Automatic smoothness \\\"Always on\\\"); Location (Use my location); \\\"Saved in this browser\\\" note; the 16-swatch scene gallery; Open-Meteo credit.\\n- [x] AC-41: On a deliberately weak test device, numbers, forecasts and Compare all work with a simpler scene.\\n  At 25× CPU slowdown the scene dropped to its still level (and frosted blur switches off) while Home's figures, both forecasts and Compare kept working normally.\\n- [x] AC-42: Home, Search and Saved cities exist with their listed contents.\\n  Home: scene, city name, conditions, 24-hour strip, 7-day list, unit toggle, search entry, refresh, last-updated. Search: box with live results, no-results and error states. Saved cities: each city's temperature and condition, remove, compare ticks, browser-only note.\\n\"}"}}]},"finish_reason":null}]}

data: {"choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"tool_calls":[{"index":2,"id":"coder_write_1","function":{"name":"write_file","arguments":""}}]},"finish_reason":null}]}

data: {"choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"tool_calls":[{"index":2,"function":{"arguments":"{\"path\":\"AGENTS.md\",\"content\":\"# Building this app\\n\\nA Bun workspace with three packages. Keep each kind of code in its place:\\n\\n- `client/`: the UI. Vite, React, React Router, TanStack Query, Tailwind CSS v4 and `@corbits/react-ui`. It talks to the server only over HTTP at `/api`.\\n- `server/`: the API. Hono on Bun. Every route lives under `/api`. Static data the app ships with goes in `server/src/data/*.json`; data people create goes through `createStore`.\\n- `packages/shared/`: zod schemas for what crosses the wire. The server builds a response from a schema and the client parses it with the same one (see `Health` and `getJson`). Nothing else goes here.\\n\\n## How screens are built\\n\\n- Data: one `useQuery` per resource in `client/src/api.ts`, fetching with `getJson` and the shared schema.\\n- Every screen that shows data renders it through `QueryState` (`client/src/components/query-state.tsx`): it shows the loading skeleton, the error with a retry button, and the empty state you pass, so none is forgotten.\\n- Changes: a `useMutation` that calls `sendJson` and, on success, invalidates the queries it changed and calls `toast(\\\"…\\\")` from `@corbits/react-ui/ui/toast`. A failed save already shows its error as a toast.\\n- The server keeps the app's data with `createStore` (`server/src/store.ts`): JSON under `server/data`, validated and written one change at a time. Routes validate request bodies with the shared schemas and answer `{ error }` with a 4xx status when they are wrong.\\n- `.builder/DESIGN.md` and the mockup in `.builder/designs` are the approved design: use its screens, copy and `data-testid` values exactly. Where the mockup fakes an interaction or a result, build the real behaviour `DESIGN.md` names.\\n\\n## How sign-in is built\\n\\nEvery app with sign-in opens with a demo account, so whoever tries the app can always sign in. `server/src/data/demo-account.json` holds its email and password, and Live Preview shows them beside the app. Once the server has its account store, seed it with one line in `server/src/index.ts`:\\n\\n```ts\\nawait seedDemoAccount({ has: (email) => /* the store has this email */, create: (account) => /* add it, hashing its password as every account's is */ });\\n```\\n\\n`seedDemoAccount` (`server/src/demo-account.ts`) creates the account on first run and leaves it as it is after. Name the demo account in `README.md`. An app without sign-in deletes `server/src/demo-account.ts` and `server/src/data/demo-account.json`.\\n\\nUse components from `@corbits/react-ui` before writing your own; its theme tokens (`bg-background`, `text-foreground`, `text-muted-foreground`, `border-border`, `bg-primary`) style everything else.\\n\\nCommands, run from the root:\\n\\n- `bun run typecheck` checks every package.\\n- `bun run build` builds the client.\\n- `bun run start` serves the built client and the API on `PORT`; `check-app` runs it for you.\\n- A package belongs to the one workspace package that imports it: `client`, `server` or `packages/shared`.\\n\\n## Checking the app\\n\\n`check-app` checks the app the way it ships. It takes up to two minutes. It:\\n\\n1. fails when `README.md` is missing, still the starter's placeholder, or names a local port or machine path, and, once a `docs/USER-MANUAL.md` is written, the same for the manual and when it shows no picture from `docs/manual/`;\\n2. runs `bun run typecheck` and `bun run build`, then `bun run start` on a free `PORT`, and waits for the server to answer;\\n3. opens `/` in a headless browser at 1280 px and 390 px, follows every same-origin link it finds, and loads each page by its own URL, the way a person opening a link or refreshing does;\\n4. once a manual is written, saves a 1280 px picture of each page it opens as `docs/manual/<page>.png` (`/` is `home.png`, `/board/new` is `board-new.png`), draws each highlight the manual names, and lists every picture it saved;\\n5. fails on an uncaught error, a console error, a failed request or any response of 400 or more, a request that leaves the app, a page with no text, content wider than the screen, a highlighted element it cannot find, or a picture the manual shows that is not there after capture.\\n\\nIt does not try features. Whether each requirement works is for you to confirm in the running app, against the acceptance checklist and the design, before you call the app done.\\n\\nThe harness runs the app's dev server while you build and reloads it as files change; `app_status` gives its address and the server log. Use the browser tools on that address to try features the way a person would.\\n\\n## The app's documents\\n\\nThe app ships `README.md`, and a `docs/USER-MANUAL.md` when the person asks for one after the build. Both describe the app as built, never what was planned and dropped. Write them once the features work, and update them whenever a later change alters what they describe:\\n\\n- `README.md`, for whoever installs and runs the app (an installer, a sysadmin, an IT person): what it is; how to run it in development and in deployment; every environment variable and any `.env` file, with what each value is and where it comes from; the seed file or first-run step, when there is one; what it needs from the systems around it (database, mail, accounts, network, credentials) and what must be declared or configured there; how to tell it is running; and how its code is laid out. A script the README or a `package.json` tells anyone to run directly starts with a shebang line and is committed executable (`chmod +x`, then `git add --chmod=+x`), so it runs as unpacked.\\n- `docs/USER-MANUAL.md`, only when the person asks for it, for the people who use it, who are not developers: clear, plain, non-technical prose, one section per screen and per task, saying what the person sees and what to do. Illustrate it with the pictures `check-app` saves, each referenced with a caption: `![The board](manual/board.png)`. To point at the area or button a step refers to, reference a picture named after its page plus a word of your own, titled `highlight:` with a CSS selector and, after a bar, the text the element shows: `![Adding a card](manual/board.new-card.png \\\"highlight: button | New card\\\")`. `check-app` captures that page with a bright rounded outline and a short label around the first visible match.\\n\\n`README.md` replaces the starter's placeholder. Neither document names a port, a host address or a path on this machine; say \\\"the address the server prints\\\" instead.\\n\\nThe app is done when every item of the build plan's acceptance checklist works in the running app, `ACCEPTANCE.md` records what you did and saw for each, `README.md` describes it, and `check-app` passes after the last change you made.\\n\\n## What stays as it is\\n\\nThese starter files already work and are what the check relies on; build around them:\\n\\n- `server/src/client.ts` serves the built client and answers every non-API path with `index.html`.\\n- `server/src/store.ts`, `client/src/components/query-state.tsx` and the `scripts` in each `package.json`.\\n\\nNever hardcode ports, hosts or absolute paths. Data the app fetches from an outside service is requested by the server or the client exactly as that service documents it.\\n\"}"}}]},"finish_reason":null}]}

data: {"choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"tool_calls":[{"index":3,"id":"coder_write_2","function":{"name":"write_file","arguments":""}}]},"finish_reason":null}]}

data: {"choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"tool_calls":[{"index":3,"function":{"arguments":"{\"path\":\"README.md\",\"content\":\"# Animated Weather\\n\\nA browser weather app whose whole screen moves with the weather. Rain falls in thousands of drops\\nwith splashes, snow drifts in depth, storms flash with lightning, wind blows clouds, leaves and\\ngrass, heat shimmers and fog rolls, each by day and by night at the city being shown. Under the\\nscene sit the usual tools: your location, city search, saved cities, °C/°F, a 24-hour and 7-day\\nforecast, and a side-by-side comparison of 2 to 4 cities. It can be installed to a phone or\\ncomputer home screen.\\n\\nThere is no sign-in and no database. Saved cities, the unit, the \\\"Simpler effects\\\" choice, the\\ncompare picks and the last weather seen per place are kept in the visitor's own browser\\n(localStorage). Clearing browser data removes them.\\n\\n## What it needs\\n\\n- [Bun](https://bun.sh) 1.3 or later.\\n- Outbound HTTPS from the server to Open-Meteo's two free services, which need no key or account:\\n  - `api.open-meteo.com` (forecast)\\n  - `geocoding-api.open-meteo.com` (city search)\\n\\n  The browser only talks to this app's own server; the server relays those two services and keeps\\n  their answers in memory for five minutes so repeat lookups don't spend the free allowance.\\n  Nothing a visitor sends is stored. Open-Meteo's free tier is for non-commercial use; a commercial\\n  deployment needs their paid plan.\\n- No environment file. The only setting is `PORT` (below).\\n\\n## Run it\\n\\n```sh\\nbun install          # once, at the repository root\\nbun run dev          # development: Vite for the client plus the Hono server, both reloading\\n```\\n\\nFor a deployment:\\n\\n```sh\\nbun install\\nbun run build        # builds the client into client/dist\\nPORT=8080 bun run start\\n```\\n\\n| Variable | Meaning | Default |\\n| -------- | ------- | ------- |\\n| `PORT`   | Port the server listens on. In development it is also where Vite sends `/api` requests. | Bun's default (3000) |\\n\\nThere is no seed step and no first-run setup.\\n\\nThe app must be served over HTTPS (or from localhost) for the browser to offer location and the\\nhome-screen install.\\n\\n## Checking it is running\\n\\n- `GET /api/health` answers `{\\\"ok\\\":true,\\\"time\\\":\\\"…\\\"}`.\\n- Opening the address the server prints shows the app; the browser asks for location on the first\\n  visit. Declining shows the \\\"Find your weather\\\" page with city search.\\n\\n## How the code is laid out\\n\\n```\\nclient/                     React app (Vite, React Router, TanStack Query, Tailwind, @corbits/react-ui)\\n  index.html                paints the last scene's colours and starts Home's weather request before the app loads\\n  public/                   web app manifest, icons, service worker (caches the app's own files only)\\n  src/main.tsx              app entry; registers the service worker in production builds\\n  src/routes.tsx            Home /, Search /search, Saved cities /cities, Compare /compare, Settings /settings\\n  src/styles.css            design tokens, per-scene sky colours and palettes, frosted panels, layouts\\n  src/api.ts                data hooks: forecast (5-minute reuse, offline copy), city search, nearest name\\n  src/components/           app shell (sidebar, phone drawer), forecast lists, places panel, small parts\\n  src/pages/                one file per screen\\n  src/lib/weather.ts        weather codes → one of 7 scenes + intensity; day/night from the city's sunrise/sunset\\n  src/lib/format.ts         units (°C/km/h/mm or °F/mph/in), times at the city, \\\"2 min ago\\\"\\n  src/lib/storage.ts        everything kept in the browser, checked on read\\n  src/lib/location.ts       browser location, asked on first open and on \\\"Use my location\\\"\\n  src/scene/                the animated canvas: engine (loop, cross-fade, pause when hidden),\\n                            quality monitor, one file per effect (rain, storm, snow, wind, cloud, fog, clear, heat)\\nserver/src/index.ts         Hono: gzip, /api/health, /api/forecast, /api/places, /api/nearest, then the built client\\nserver/src/openMeteo.ts     relays Open-Meteo with a short in-memory cache\\npackages/shared/src/        zod schemas for the Open-Meteo answers the client parses\\n```\\n\\n### Scene test route\\n\\n`/?force=<condition>[-light|-moderate|-heavy][-day|-night]` shows Home with a forced scene, for\\nexample `/?force=rain-heavy-night` or `/?force=storm-day`. Conditions: `clear`, `hot`, `cloudy`,\\n`rain`, `storm`, `snow`, `wind`, `fog`. The canvas reports its state in data attributes\\n(`data-quality` 0–3, `data-particles`, `data-fps`, `data-running`, `data-max-flashes-per-second`).\\n\\n## Commands\\n\\n- `bun run typecheck` checks every package.\\n- `bun run build` builds the client.\\n- `bun run start` serves the built client and the API on `PORT`.\\n\"}"}}]},"finish_reason":null}]}

data: {"choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"tool_calls":[{"index":4,"id":"coder_write_3","function":{"name":"write_file","arguments":""}}]},"finish_reason":null}]}

data: {"choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"tool_calls":[{"index":4,"function":{"arguments":"{\"path\":\"client/index.html\",\"content\":\"<!doctype html>\\n<html lang=\\\"en\\\">\\n  <head>\\n    <meta charset=\\\"UTF-8\\\" />\\n    <meta name=\\\"viewport\\\" content=\\\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0, viewport-fit=cover\\\" />\\n    <title>Animated Weather</title>\\n    <meta name=\\\"description\\\" content=\\\"Weather you can feel: the whole screen moves with the weather where you are.\\\" />\\n    <meta name=\\\"theme-color\\\" content=\\\"#cfd9e4\\\" />\\n    <link rel=\\\"manifest\\\" href=\\\"/manifest.webmanifest\\\" />\\n    <link rel=\\\"icon\\\" href=\\\"/icon.svg\\\" type=\\\"image/svg+xml\\\" />\\n    <link rel=\\\"apple-touch-icon\\\" href=\\\"/icon-192.png\\\" />\\n    <meta name=\\\"apple-mobile-web-app-capable\\\" content=\\\"yes\\\" />\\n    <meta name=\\\"apple-mobile-web-app-title\\\" content=\\\"Weather\\\" />\\n    <script>\\n      // Paint the last scene's colours before the app loads, so there is no white flash.\\n      try {\\n        var s = JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem(\\\"aw.scene\\\") || \\\"null\\\");\\n        var k = s && typeof s.key === \\\"string\\\" ? s.key : \\\"calm\\\";\\n        document.documentElement.dataset.wx = k;\\n        document.documentElement.classList.add(/-night$/.test(k) ? \\\"dark\\\" : \\\"light\\\");\\n      } catch (e) {\\n        document.documentElement.dataset.wx = \\\"calm\\\";\\n      }\\n      // Start fetching Home's weather while the app itself downloads, so figures appear sooner.\\n      try {\\n        if (location.pathname === \\\"/\\\") {\\n          var read = function (k) { try { return JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem(k) || \\\"null\\\"); } catch (e) { return null; } };\\n          var q = new URLSearchParams(location.search);\\n          var st = read(\\\"aw.settings\\\") || {};\\n          var here = read(\\\"aw.here\\\");\\n          var cities = read(\\\"aw.cities\\\") || [];\\n          var find = function (id) { for (var i = 0; i < cities.length; i++) if (cities[i].id === id) return cities[i]; return null; };\\n          var p = null;\\n          if (q.get(\\\"lat\\\") && q.get(\\\"lon\\\")) p = { lat: +q.get(\\\"lat\\\"), lon: +q.get(\\\"lon\\\") };\\n          else if (q.get(\\\"city\\\") === \\\"here\\\") p = here;\\n          else if (q.get(\\\"city\\\")) p = find(q.get(\\\"city\\\"));\\n          else if (here && st.location === \\\"granted\\\") p = here;\\n          else if (st.lastPlace) p = find(st.lastPlace);\\n          if (p && isFinite(p.lat) && isFinite(p.lon)) {\\n            var url = \\\"/api/forecast?lat=\\\" + p.lat.toFixed(2) + \\\"&lon=\\\" + p.lon.toFixed(2);\\n            var response = fetch(url);\\n            response.catch(function () {});\\n            window.__prefetch = { url: url, response: response };\\n          }\\n        }\\n      } catch (e) {}\\n    </script>\\n  </head>\\n  <body>\\n    <div id=\\\"root\\\"></div>\\n    <script type=\\\"module\\\" src=\\\"/src/main.tsx\\\"></script>\\n  </body>\\n</html>\\n\"}"}}]},"finish_reason":null}]}

